#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198153 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36fa0f2f-3812-521a-aca3-900556be84ff" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Non-Rectangular Sets Using Set-Builder Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "88fba9fd-d05b-546c-89aa-edcf8d2fc93e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13158" :question/problem "Use set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f5f2f2e67ba0ad566b2759ca5536d248f671bf0f082d567a7f7d993772601e.png)" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=5$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the ellipse, say, $(10,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(10)^2+25(0)^2 \\: {\\color{blue}>} \\: 400.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\gt 400.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16x^2 + 25y^2 = 400$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16x^2 + 25y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\Big\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\: : \\: \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 }x^2 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25 }y^2  \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\geq } 400 \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8cade90-de90-5cc5-a96f-4b62957e1afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121332} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a034d-0611-5f21-a47f-fb41d7d10986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121335} {:answer/id #uuid "72b6d84b-4599-5ae1-87e9-228fcb3fc5f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426052} {:answer/id #uuid "ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426060} {:answer/id #uuid "c784e28a-945a-50b9-a3c3-88323f6f5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\mathbb{R}}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426060} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cf5fde-aae3-5ac2-808d-cd031ad46138" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b33961fd-1191-51c9-841c-0bdd04b0318b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140766" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{n} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879514d5-e127-5485-ab41-d459f6b5f379" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e2978f-44c4-5ccc-a792-625eb51d9546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269420} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0c5b92-9bb1-5e5e-b9ec-edf4c0497369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{n}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270474} {:answer/id #uuid "9d9fdfe6-8dc9-510f-9c18-69a49e29297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270666} {:answer/id #uuid "c3310529-53bb-5e2d-a3be-0786dc03cb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{\\mathbf{1}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270996} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8902d1-b96f-5746-b901-b960abac5e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d9fdfe6-8dc9-510f-9c18-69a49e29297b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270666} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b2b2ef-efa9-59ec-95ce-60b79c07943a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a09c603d-a3ea-5c23-b944-39ca22d1abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141192" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=\\underline{\\phantom{n}}=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &={\\color{blue}n}=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3b9100-91b5-59ec-8676-832893b05ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20903be6-89e3-5119-ab2f-761f0d0da1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20903be6-89e3-5119-ab2f-761f0d0da1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660494} :answer-field/id #uuid "1465d378-1afb-5561-9949-861532da7c31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d057c7-4c76-57ee-ad78-fa96cae384aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d057c7-4c76-57ee-ad78-fa96cae384aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660675} :answer-field/id #uuid "9172dc51-97c6-5bfc-9571-88a20012b26e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccbaa794-afb5-5200-b91c-e8c3a1f38e0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-280352" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}=⟨1,5,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}=⟨-2,4,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}).$\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,0⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a-3b)=c\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)=2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b) & =2⟨1,5,0⟩-3⟨-2,4,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10,0⟩+⟨6,-12,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d11f13f0-1cfa-59ec-a04a-9483d942b1aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equation of the Plane Passing Through Three Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336bb496-d1df-5e9a-a07a-1ec951355ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The plane is undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302774} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8dad69-2acf-5472-9a3f-505a95ac3fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+s⟨1,4,-14⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303341} {:answer/id #uuid "b208b020-2e95-5012-90d1-880d0704ce1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303557} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a6125d-748b-50ac-93e6-06be995d0638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-19⟩+s⟨-2,-2,-2⟩+t⟨1,2,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303769} {:answer/id #uuid "feb8b07e-b16f-57b2-afdf-a8e6076cfea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb8b07e-b16f-57b2-afdf-a8e6076cfea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304058} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a308e92-1035-59ed-9244-1f44706e0efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "133e1de3-de48-5298-8948-a57726f1d155" :question/math-academy-id "q-33160" :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,4,-14)$ and is parallel to the plane determined by the two distinct parallel lines $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,5,7⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨0,3,-19⟩+t⟨-2,-2,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The two lines have direction vectors $\\mathbf d_1=\\langle1,1,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf d_2=\\langle-2,-2,-2\\rangle=-2\\mathbf d_1$, so they are parallel. Their base points are $P(-1,5,7)$ and $Q(0,3,-19)$. The vector between them is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf u=\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle1,-2,-26\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe plane determined by the two lines has direction span $\\operatorname{span}\\{\\mathbf d_1,\\mathbf u\\}$. These vectors are independent because $\\mathbf d_1\\times\\mathbf u=\\langle-24,27,-3\\rangle\\ne\\mathbf0$. A parallel plane through $C(1,4,-14)$ has the same direction span, hence its parametric equation is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle1,4,-14\\rangle+s\\langle1,1,1\\rangle+t\\langle1,-2,-26\\rangle,\\qquad s,t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nThis is the unique listed plane with that direction span passing through $C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb90e5b9-03fe-51cf-bc16-a293befacebc" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bec134f4-8ac1-5fd3-a994-79031801df9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123603} {:answer/id #uuid "e52ece27-d671-527d-aac5-dac055efd06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123604} {:answer/id #uuid "2e01b189-5d9a-582b-a3da-d616b84586a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123605} {:answer/id #uuid "32960b14-b04a-59a2-9224-0b8a82626700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123606} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffd9fe3-d1f5-5a30-b5aa-c6b01c0e6506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e01b189-5d9a-582b-a3da-d616b84586a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123605} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eda024b-b844-536e-bf1a-f1efd2375bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123602}] :question/id #uuid "f935b331-7ef7-5abb-b2c6-c78c4160f9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-47586" :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $n \\times n$ matrix and $\\det(A) \\ne 0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $A$ has no inverse\n2. $\\operatorname{Null}(A)$ has the empty set as a basis\n3. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\det(A)\\ne0$, the matrix $A$ is invertible. Thus statement I is false.\n\nThe homogeneous equation $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf0$ has only the trivial solution, so $\\operatorname{Null}(A)=\\{\\mathbf0\\}$. The empty set is linearly independent and its span is $\\{\\mathbf0\\}$, so it is a basis of this null space. Thus statement II is true.\n\nAn invertible $n\\times n$ matrix has a pivot in every column, so statement III is true. Therefore, the correct answer is II and III only."}]}
